9:## Individualized functional connectome abnormalities obtained using two normative model unveil neurophysiological subtypes of obsessive compulsive disorder
29:The high heterogeneity observed among patients with obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) underscores the need to identify neurophysiological OCD subtypes to facilitate personalized diagnosis and treatment. In this study, our aim was to identify potential OCD subtypes based on individualized functional connectome abnormalities. We recruited a total of 99 patients with OCD and 104 healthy controls (HCs) matched for demographic characteristics. Individualized functional connectome abnormalities were obtained using normative models of functional connectivity strength (FCS) and used as features to unveil OCD subtypes. Sensitivity analyses were conducted to assess the reproducibility and robustness of the clustering outcomes. Patients exhibited significant intersubject heterogeneity in individualized functional connectome abnormalities. Two subtypes with distinct patterns of FCS abnormalities relative to HCs were identified. Subtype 1 patients primarily exhibited significantly decreased FCS in regions including the frontal gyrus, insula, hippocampus, and precentral/postcentral gyrus, whereas subtype 2 patients demonstrated increased FCS in widespread brain regions. When all patients were combined, no significant differences were observed. Additionally, the identified subtypes showed significant differences in age of onset. Furthermore, sensitivity analyses confirmed the reproducibility of our subtyping results. In conclusion, the identified OCD subtypes shed new light on the taxonomy and neurophysiological heterogeneity of OCD.
45:poses a significant obstacle in neuroimaging studies, hindering the identification of reproducible biomarkers for clinical decision-making (Boedhoe et al., 2018; Saxena and Rauch, 2000). Most neuroimaging studies still rely on traditional case-control approaches, which only capture effects observable at the group level. It ' s becoming increasingly clear that such approaches may miss neuroimaging characteristics unique to individuals (Lv et al., 2021a; Marquand et al., 2016; Wolfers et al., 2018). To address this heterogeneity, the normative model has been proposed (Marquand et al., 2016). This approach infers individualized neuroimaging abnormalities by assessing deviations from the normal distribution (Marquand et al., 2016). Using the normative model, neuroimaging studies have successfully identified subject-level structural brain alterations in mental disorders such as autism, schizophrenia, and depression (Han et al., 2023a; Wolfers and Beckmann, 2020; Wolfers et al., 2018; Zabihi et al., 2019). These individual deviations from the normative model can inform neurobiological subtypes with distinct clinical manifestations in psychiatric disorders (Sun et al., 2023).
47:In this study, we conducted a comprehensive investigation into the neurobiological heterogeneity and subtypes of OCD using a dataset comprising 99 patients with OCD and 104 healthy controls. We obtained individualized functional connectome abnormalities, measured with functional connectivity strength (FCS), using the normative model framework. Subsequently, we identified potential subtypes based on individualized FCS abnormalities as features. Finally, we evaluated differences in clinical variables and functional connectomes between the identified OCD subtypes.
75:First, we evaluated the performance of the Gaussian process regression model in predicting FCS values within HCs through 10-fold crossvalidation. The standardized mean squared error (MSE) between the true FCS values and predicted values was computed to assess the model ' s accuracy (Marquand et al., 2016). Subsequently, for each brain region, a Gaussian process regression model was trained to estimate the normative range of FCS values as a function of age and sex within HCs. This model was then applied to each patient, yielding a predicted FCS value. A Z -score was calculated to quantify the deviation of predicted FCS value from the normal distribution. A positive Z-score indicated a higher FCS compared to HCs, while a negative Z-score indicated the opposite. This process resulted in a Z-score vector (246 × 1) for each patient, representing individualized FCS abnormalities.
81:percentage of participants exhibiting extreme deviations, defined as Zscores exceeding ± 1.96 (thus surpassing the two-tailed 95% confidence interval), thereby delineating extreme positive or negative deviations from the normative model (Haas et al., 2024; Sun et al., 2023).
119:The 10-fold cross-validation performed on HCs demonstrated a high level of generalizability in the fitting performance of normative models for FCS, as evidenced by a mean MSE close to 1 (99.94e GLYPH&lt;0&gt; 2 ± 5.84e GLYPH&lt;0&gt; 2 ). We also examined the distribution of Z scores across brain regions and subjects and found most of them fall between GLYPH&lt;0&gt; 1.96 and 1.96. The spatial distribution of MSE is depicted in Fig. S1 and Z scores across brain regions and subjects is depicted in Fig. S2. The differential pattern of Z scores between patients with OCD and HCs was showed in Fig. S3.
121:Following confirmation of the normative model ' s performance, we applied the trained model derived from HCs to patients with OCD to ascertain individualized FCS abnormalities. Patients with OCD exhibited heterogeneous patterns of individualized FCS abnormalities. Specifically, 44.44% ( n = 44) of OCD patients demonstrated extreme negative deviations from the normative model in at least one brain region. These extreme negative deviations were predominantly observed in regions such as the precentral and postcentral gyrus, superior medial frontal gyrus, ventromedial prefrontal cortex, superior temporal gyrus, thalamus, insula, and hippocampus (Fig. 1). Additionally, 21.21% ( n = 21) of OCD patients displayed extreme positive deviations from the normative model in at least one brain region, spanning across various regions of the brain (Fig. 1). However, the percentage of patients exhibiting extreme deviations from the normative range in any single brain region was notably low, whether in positive or negative deviations (Fig. 1). These findings underscore the substantial heterogeneity in individualized FCS abnormalities among patients with OCD.
129:Fig. 1. The spatial overlap maps depict patients with OCD exhibiting extreme deviations from the normative range. The left panel illustrates extreme positive deviations, while the right panel illustrates extreme negative deviations.
175:Patients with OCD demonstrated heterogenous in individualized functional connectome abnormalities. The use of normative models has become increasingly prevalent in addressing intersubject heterogeneity of structural neuroimaging metrics, such as cortical thickness and gray/ white matter morphology, particularly in heterogeneous disorders like mental disorders (Han et al., 2023a; Li et al., 2024; Marquand et al., 2016). Unlike traditional statistical models focusing on group-level effects, normative models do not necessitate assumptions about change trajectories and provide statistical inferences at the individual level. A recent study affirmed the efficacy of this model in predicting FCS across multiple datasets with large sample sizes (Sun et al., 2023). Prior to applying the normative model to patients, we first evaluated its feasibility in our own center ' s dataset. Consistent with previous findings, the normative model demonstrated reliable performance in predicting FCS values in HCs. Patients with OCD exhibited both increased and decreased individualized FCS in distributed brain regions, including the precentral and postcentral gyrus, ventromedial prefrontal cortex, thalamus, insula, and hippocampus, all of which have been implicated in the pathophysiology of OCD (Piras et al., 2015). For instance, previous studies have reported larger volumes in the precentral/postcentral gyrus among patients with OCD (Kim et al., 2020; Rus et al., 2017), along with documented functional connectivity alterations in this region (Lulu et al., 2023). Dysfunction of the precentral/postcentral gyrus has been linked to abnormalities in habit control, goal-directed behavior, and sensorimotor gating in OCD (Ahmari et al., 2012; Gillan et al., 2011; Hoenig et al., 2005; Jahanshahi et al., 2015; Voon et al., 2020). However, we found the overlap rates among patients win these regions were very low. However, we observed low overlap rates among patients within these regions, consistent with findings from studies on individualized gray matter morphological abnormalities in psychiatric disorders (Segal and Parkes, 2023; Wolfers and Beckmann, 2020; Wolfers et al., 2018; Zabihi et al., 2019). Taken together, these results suggest that alterations in FCS represent an important neuropathological feature of OCD, indicating the presence of multiple subtypes within the disorder.
177:Individualized functional connectome abnormalities uncover two OCD subtypes with opposite differential patterns. Patients with subtype 1 mainly showed decreased FCS in the frontal gyrus, insula, hippocampus and precentral/postcentral gyrus while patients with subtype 2 demonstrated increased FCS in widespread brain regions. Previous studies investigating functional connectome abnormalities in OCD have reported inconsistent or even conflicting findings. For instance, Lv et al. identified increased functional connectivity in the thalamus and decreased functional connectivity in the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex and precuneus (Lv et al., 2021b), while Shan et al. only observed decreased functional connectivity in the temporal gyrus (Shan et al., 2019). Interestingly, in our study, we did not detect any significant differences in functional connectome when considering all patients as a single group. This incongruity among findings could potentially be attributed to the varying proportions of the two subtypes within the sample. In clinical practice, patients with OCD are usually further divided into various subtypes according to symptom profiles (e.g. checking and washing), disease courses (e.g. early-onset and late-onset) or treatment responses (normal and refractory) (Mataix-Cols et al., 2004). Subtypes based on symptomatology are found to distinctly response to treatment (Starcevic and Brakoulias, 2008). Although promising, symptom-based subtyping has some limitations as we mentioned before. Recent years, there a trend to identify subtypes from neuroimaging metrics. In our prior studies, we have also found similar subtypes with discrepancies in structural abnormalities in OCD and other psychiatric disorders (Han et al., 2023a; Han et al., 2022a). However, these relevant studies also suffer limitations. First, psychiatric disorders including OCD are brain connectome disorders (Fornito et al., 2015; Kaiser et al., 2015), while most of them just use structural or functional metrics as subtyping features, ignoring connectomes between brain regions (Ding et al., 2024; Xu et al., 2023). Second, these studies identify subtypes directly using neuroimaging features but not diseaserelated neuroimaging features (differences from normal population). In this study, we unveiled subtypes from individualized FCS differences by leveraging a newly proposed method (normative model).As a result, we succeed in identifying two subtypes with opposite patterns of abnormal FCS compared with HCs.
