51:## 2.1. Participants
57:Healthy controls (HCs, n = 104) were enlisted from the community via poster advertisements. All participants were of Han Chinese ethnicity, right-handed, and underwent screening to ensure they met specified exclusion criteria. These criteria included abstaining from medications such as anesthetics and analgesics within the preceding month, no history of substance abuse, brain tumors, traumatic brain injury, surgeries, or organic body diseases, as well as the absence of cardiovascular diseases, diabetes, or hypertension. Additionally, individuals with contraindications for MRI scanning or other structural brain abnormalities were excluded from the study.
67:The functional images underwent preprocessing using Data Processing &amp; Analysis for Brain Imaging (DPABI, http://rfmri.org/dpabi) (Dong et al., 2023a; Yan et al., 2016). This involved several steps: removal of the first 10 volumes, slice timing correction, and realignment. Subsequently, the images were normalized to the standard EPI template, resampled into a voxel size of 3 × 3 × 3 mm 3 . Subjects were excluded if their translational or rotational displacement exceeded 3.0 mm or 3.0 ◦ . None of subjects was excluded according to this standard. The images were then smoothed using a Gaussian kernel with a full width at half maximum (FWHM) of 6 × 6 × 6 mm 3 and detrended to mitigate low-frequency drift. A band-pass filter (0.01 -0.1 Hz) was applied to remove high-frequency physiological noise. Friston 24 motion parameters (Satterthwaite et al., 2012), white matter signal and cerebrospinal fluid signal were regressed out as nuisance covariates. Specially, the global signal was not regressed in the current study for the reason that the variance of global signal along with its topography were found altered mental disease (Han et al., 2019; Yang et al., 2014; Zhang et al., 2019a). Additionally, despiking was employed to replace outliers with the best estimate using a third-order spline fit, based on the detection of outliers using the median absolute deviation method, as implemented in 3dDespike (http://afni.nimh.nih.gov/afni) (Allen et al., 2014). The mean frame-wise displacement (FD) was also calculated to assess its effects on our results (see below)(Dong et al., 2023b; Han et al., 2020; Han et al., 2018; Wang et al., 2023).
75:First, we evaluated the performance of the Gaussian process regression model in predicting FCS values within HCs through 10-fold crossvalidation. The standardized mean squared error (MSE) between the true FCS values and predicted values was computed to assess the model ' s accuracy (Marquand et al., 2016). Subsequently, for each brain region, a Gaussian process regression model was trained to estimate the normative range of FCS values as a function of age and sex within HCs. This model was then applied to each patient, yielding a predicted FCS value. A Z -score was calculated to quantify the deviation of predicted FCS value from the normal distribution. A positive Z-score indicated a higher FCS compared to HCs, while a negative Z-score indicated the opposite. This process resulted in a Z-score vector (246 × 1) for each patient, representing individualized FCS abnormalities.
89:Subsequently, we derived distinct patterns of FCS within each subtype and across all patients using a two-sample t -test while controlling for age, sex, and mean FD. Significance was established at p &lt; 0.05 after Bonferroni correction. Additionally, we explored demographic and clinical disparities among the identified subtypes, encompassing age, sex, educational level, symptom severity as measured by the total score of the Y-BOCS, illness duration, and age of onset. To assess whether head motion influenced the subtyping outcomes, we compared mean FD between the identified subtypes. This analysis aimed to ascertain the impact of motion artifacts on subtype differentiation.
